      <body>
         <div type="edition" xml:lang="pra-Latn" rendition="class:83225 maturity:83209">
             <p><lb n="1"/><gap reason="lost"/>sa bhaḍākārikasa mak<unclear>o</unclear>samasa dāna </p>
         </div>
         <div type="apparatus">
             <listApp>
                 <app loc="1">
                     <lem><gap reason="lost"/>sa bhaḍākārikasa</lem>
                     <rdg source="bib:Nakanishi+Hinuber2014_01">sabhaḍākārikasa</rdg>
                     <note>The editors note: <quote>It is not certain, but likely that the beginning of the inscription is preserved. Sabhaḍākāra might be a place name.</quote> However, if one takes the first <foreign>sa</foreign> as the Gen. Sg. ending of a lost word, <foreign>bhaḍākārika</foreign> may be satisfactorily understood on its own as a straightforward equivalent of Skt. <foreign xml:lang="san-Latn">bhaṇḍāgārika</foreign>, meaning “keeper of the storehouse.” This title may be either a monastic title—attested for instance in a list of 13 offices in the Cullavagga of the Pāli Vinaya—or an administrative one. See, respectively, <bibl><ptr target="bib:Silk2008_01"/><citedRange unit="page">106</citedRange><citedRange unit="page">171</citedRange></bibl>; <bibl><ptr target="bib:Sircar1966_01"/></bibl>, s.v. <foreign xml:lang="san-Latn">bhaṇḍāgārika</foreign>. As far as I know, this is the earliest epigraphic instance of this title. Unfortunately, the present context does not allow us to understand whether the donor was a monk or a royal officer. Cf. <bibl><ptr target="bib:Tournier2020_02"/><citedRange unit="page">880</citedRange><citedRange unit="note">66</citedRange></bibl>.</note>
                 </app>
                 <app loc="1">
                     <lem><gap reason="lost"/>mak<unclear>o</unclear>samasa</lem>
                     <note>The element <foreign>maka-</foreign> is also met as first element of a proper name, <foreign>makabudhi</foreign> in <ref target="DHARMA_INSEIAD00305">EIAD 305</ref>.</note>
                 </app>
             </listApp>
         </div>
         <div type="translation" resp="part:vito">
             <p/>
         </div>
